{"kind":"request","text":"Claude: make a recut republishable with one command.\n\nWhy. gs review-request refuses when the promise carries live artifacts of mine at an earlier head (cmd/gs/review_request.go requireOneHead, main 3c7f13c7): \"promise also carries live artifacts at another head; gs review refuses a mixed-head set, so republish every path at \u003chead\u003e\". Every repair round and every recut onto a moved main produces exactly that state, because gs artifact at the new head leaves the earlier head's artifacts live. The fix the refusal names does not clear it: republishing adds artifacts at the new head and the old ones stay live. Resolving the Dependabot runtime lane on 2026-09-17 took a hand-written gs supersede per old-head artifact before the review request would file, and an old-head artifact left live is later sealed by the merge as a sibling or abandoned, which is a cleanup obligation the merge cannot discharge.\n\nOutcome. gs artifact at a new head on a promise that already carries live artifacts of the same actor at an earlier head retires those earlier artifacts in the same batch: each path the new head also changes gets the new artifact as its successor, and a path the new head no longer changes is retired bare. The fold already admits both from the artifact's own author. The result is one live head per promise, so gs review-request finds one head with nothing to clean up. gs review-request keeps its refusal for a set that is still mixed, and its message names gs artifact as the repair only when that repair works.","body":{"branch":"request/recut-republish","conditions":"1. gs artifact --head \u003cnew\u003e --promise \u003cp\u003e \u003cpaths\u003e on a promise with live same-actor artifacts at an earlier head retires each of them in the same signed batch, as the successor of the new artifact at the same path where that path recurs, and bare otherwise; the command prints what it retired. 2. It never retires another actor's artifact, an artifact at the same head, or one resting on a different promise; a test pins each. 3. A test shows the recut sequence end to end: publish at head A, publish at head B on the same promise, gs review-request --head B succeeds with no manual supersede, and the merge plan for B seals nothing from head A as abandoned. 4. gs review-request's mixed-head refusal remains for a set the actor cannot repair this way and its message says what to do. 5. docs/reference/gs/artifact.md and review-request.md describe the retirement; SKILL.md step 4 gets at most one sentence. 6. go test ./... green at the exact head; independent review by codex; ratified; landed on main and pushed.","target_head":"3c7f13c79cb079ef0f40961d51d7ff4f350787c5","target_ref":"refs/heads/main","target_repo":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}